---
/*
  QuoteCard — one quote in the blog feed. Quotes are short, so they render whole
  (no reader). When a search term is active, matches in the quote and its
  attribution highlight inline (search-highlight.ts — same engine as the essays;
  short fields use highlight(), not windowed excerpts, per docs/search.md).

  Layout notes (deliberate):
    · The quote is NOT italicized — a full italic block at this size reads as
      strained; upright serif is easier on the eyes. Set light, at reading
      size, with line breaks preserved (poetry survives).
    · The attribution is serif ALL-SMALL-CAPS in muted ink — the same
      convention the constellation suite uses (sky-lab round 1; Michael's
      call 2026-07-23: the blog follows the Sky here, not the reverse).
      Subjects + the date stay sans — they're chrome, not content.
    · The attribution is also the CITATION REVEAL when there's something behind
      it (QuoteReveal, chosen from the reveal-lab bench 2026-08-05). Nothing is added to
      the line — no glyph, no icon — because every candidate that added one made
      the quietest line on the site louder. See the component for the cost.
    · The date is intentionally last, after the tags, and labelled "Added …":
      a quote's date is provenance (when it entered Michael's life), NOT when
      it was said — putting a bare date next to the attribution wrongly
      implies the latter.
*/
import Reveal from './Reveal.astro';
import FragmentStrip from './FragmentStrip.astro';
import Highlighted from './admin/Highlighted.astro';
import { MIN_SEARCH } from '../lib/search-highlight';
import { renderMarkdown } from '../lib/markdown';
import type { QuoteItem } from '../lib/blog';

interface Props {
  item: QuoteItem;
  term?: string;
}
const { item, term = '' } = Astro.props;
const searching = term.trim().length >= MIN_SEARCH;

// ⚠ MARKDOWN, AND HIGHLIGHTED INSIDE THE RENDER — see QuoteArticle for why the
// body is rendered at all, and `renderMarkdown`'s `highlight` option for why the
// marking cannot happen afterwards.
//
// This is the ONLY surface on the site that renders a full body AND highlights
// it: an essay card highlights its `lede`, which is already a `toPlainText`
// projection (blog.ts), so the question never came up there. Passing the term
// down instead of wrapping the output in `<Highlighted>` is what keeps the
// escaping promise — every segment is escaped on its own, exactly as that
// component does it, and the attribution below still goes through it unchanged.
const bodyHtml = renderMarkdown(item.body, { breaks: true, highlight: searching ? term : undefined });

// ⚠ The attribution is a control when there is ANYTHING behind it — a citation,
// or the rest of that author's lines. Until 2026-08-10 the feed offered only the
// citation, so a plainly-quoted person was a dead end here while the same quote's
// own page offered the door. One rule, both surfaces (plan 32 · §5).
const hasDoor = Boolean(item.author && item.authorSiblings > 0);
const authorHref = item.author ? `/blog?view=quotes&author=${item.author.slug}` : null;
---

<style>
  /* A warm, not an underline — see the note on the anchor. The trace at rest is
     what tells a reader who never moves the pointer that these go somewhere,
     the same two-intensity trick `.sky-name` uses on the overview. */
  .quote-line blockquote {
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-base-content) 96%, var(--color-primary));
    transition: color 0.3s ease;
  }
  @media (hover: hover) {
    .quote-line:hover blockquote {
      color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-base-content) 78%, var(--color-primary));
    }
  }
  .quote-line:focus-visible {
    outline: 1px dotted currentColor;
    outline-offset: 6px;
    border-radius: 2px;
  }
</style>

<figure class="py-9 first:pt-0">
  {
    /*
    ⚠ THE LINE IS THE LINK, and it is the only chrome-free way to reach a quote's
    own page (plan 32 · §3, added with §9). Until that page existed, a quote card
    was the one item in this feed that led nowhere and correctly had no
    affordance saying otherwise. Now it leads somewhere, and something has to say
    so.

    Every alternative was louder. A permalink glyph on the caption is the thing
    QuoteReveal was chosen specifically to avoid — "every candidate that added
    one made the quietest line on the site louder". Turning the "Added" date into
    the link would have cost the date's own popover, and nested a link inside a
    button. Making the whole <figure> clickable would have swallowed the
    attribution, which is already a control.

    So it follows the card next door: PostCard's TITLE is the link to its essay,
    and a quote's equivalent of a title is the line itself. The hover is a warm
    rather than an underline — at 1.35rem across a page of twenty-five, an
    underline is a page of underlines.

    ⚠ THIS IS THE FEED ONLY. A quote stanza in a constellation suite stays inert
    by deliberation (ConstellationSuite's header): there, the suite is the thing
    being read and a quote is a movement in it, not a card that leads away.
  */
  }
  {
    /*
    ⚠ `data-read` — IT OPENS THE SHEET, IT DOES NOT NAVIGATE (2026-08-10).
    Michael: *"when we click a quote, just how there's a reading viewer sheet
    for the writing on the blog, we should also have a sheet for the quote. and
    getting to a dedicated page should follow a similar path as the writing."*

    Until now this was the one place on the site where clicking a fragment threw
    the reader out of the list they were reading — a constellation's quote had
    opened a sheet since earlier the same day, and an essay card always had. The
    `href` is what makes "a similar path as the writing" literally true: the
    Reader's own capture handler lets ⌘/Ctrl/Shift-click and any non-primary
    button fall through to the permalink, and it IS the no-JS floor.
  */
  }
  <a href={`/blog/${item.slug}`} data-read={item.slug} class="quote-line block">
    <blockquote class="quote-body font-serif text-[1.35rem] leading-[1.5] font-light" set:html={bodyHtml} />
  </a>
  {
    item.attribution && (
      <figcaption class="text-base-content/50 mt-3 font-serif text-[1.05rem] tracking-[0.06em] [font-variant:all-small-caps]">
        {/* The em-dash stays OUTSIDE the control: it is punctuation, not the
            name, and underlining it on hover reads as a typo. */}
        {'— '}
        {item.reveal || hasDoor ? (
          <Reveal label={`About ${item.attribution}`}>
            {searching ? <Highlighted text={item.attribution} term={term} /> : item.attribution}
            <Fragment slot="content">
              {item.reveal && <span class="qp-cite">{item.reveal}</span>}
              {hasDoor && authorHref && (
                <a href={authorHref} class:list={['qp-door', item.reveal && 'qp-door--ruled']}>
                  {item.authorSiblings} more from {item.author!.name}
                  <span class="qp-door__arrow">→</span>
                </a>
              )}
            </Fragment>
          </Reveal>
        ) : searching ? (
          <Highlighted text={item.attribution} term={term} />
        ) : (
          item.attribution
        )}
      </figcaption>
    )
  }

  {
    /*
    ⚠ THE CARD CLOSES LIKE THE QUOTE DOES (ADR-0023). The subjects and the date
    used to be crammed onto the attribution line, right-aligned against it —
    a third answer to "how does a fragment present its metadata", next to the
    writing card's bare list and the opened view's ruled strip.

    The date lives here rather than in the head, because a quote card HAS no
    head: the line is the content, and "Added Wednesday" is filing.

    No related/constellation controls — see FragmentStrip. Twenty-five of them
    down this view is a toolbar, and they belong to the opened quote.
  */
  }
  <FragmentStrip
    slug={item.slug}
    subjects={item.subjects}
    occurredAt={item.occurredAt}
    precision={item.precision}
    ruled={false}
    share={false}
    shareLabel="Share this quote"
    dateKind="added"
    datePrefix="Added"
    subjectView="quotes"
  />
</figure>
